1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:05,140
Leave a cup of coffee on your desk,
and it cools quickly at first,

2
00:00:05,190 --> 00:00:09,140
then more and more slowly,
as if it's losing interest.

3
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:12,710
The hotter it is compared to the room,
the faster it cools.

4
00:00:12,790 --> 00:00:16,500
How fast something changes depends
on how much of it there is.

5
00:00:16,990 --> 00:00:18,910
That rule turns up everywhere.

6
00:00:18,960 --> 00:00:24,360
Money earning interest on its interest.
Bacteria, where every cell splits in two.

7
00:00:24,410 --> 00:00:25,670
Radioactive rock,

8
00:00:25,720 --> 00:00:29,830
where every atom has the same small
chance of decaying each second.

9
00:00:29,890 --> 00:00:34,870
Different worlds, one equation,
and one answer: e to the k t.

10
00:00:35,380 --> 00:00:37,860
People found that answer
by following money.

11
00:00:37,930 --> 00:00:41,810
In sixteen eighty-three,
Jacob Bernoulli asked a banker's question.

12
00:00:41,860 --> 00:00:44,820
If an account pays a hundred
percent a year, what happens

13
00:00:44,860 --> 00:00:49,490
when the interest is added twice a year,
then monthly, then every instant?

14
00:00:49,540 --> 00:00:51,670
The balance doesn't run off to infinity.

15
00:00:51,720 --> 00:00:55,510
It creeps toward a ceiling,
about two point seven one eight.

16
00:00:55,560 --> 00:00:58,510
Euler later gave that number its letter:
e.

17
00:00:59,090 --> 00:01:02,570
But real things rarely change alone.
Swallow a pill.

18
00:01:02,620 --> 00:01:04,230
The medicine seeps from your stomach

19
00:01:04,280 --> 00:01:07,260
into your blood, while your body
clears it out of the blood.

20
00:01:07,310 --> 00:01:11,240
How much is in your blood depends on how
much is still waiting in your stomach.

21
00:01:11,290 --> 00:01:13,480
Two amounts, tangled together.

22
00:01:13,950 --> 00:01:16,960
Heat moving between rooms,
currents in a circuit,

23
00:01:17,010 --> 00:01:21,300
a tall building swaying in the wind:
they all tangle the same way.

24
00:01:21,350 --> 00:01:24,680
Write down how they change,
and you get one shape of equation.

25
00:01:24,730 --> 00:01:28,680
The rate of change of a list of numbers
is a matrix, times that list.

26
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:32,580
Next to the one-number version,
the resemblance is hard to ignore.

27
00:01:32,630 --> 00:01:35,140
There, the answer was e to the k t.

28
00:01:35,190 --> 00:01:38,790
So it's tempting to say
the answer here is e to the t A.

29
00:01:38,840 --> 00:01:41,290
e, raised to the power of a matrix.

30
00:01:41,340 --> 00:01:44,330
Which, if you stop and think,
means nothing at all.

31
00:01:44,380 --> 00:01:48,080
You can't multiply e by itself
a grid of numbers times.

32
00:01:48,530 --> 00:01:50,500
Mathematicians wrote it down anyway.

33
00:01:50,550 --> 00:01:54,170
Edmond Laguerre put the exponential
of a matrix in print in eighteen

34
00:01:54,220 --> 00:01:56,440
sixty-seven, as an infinite series.

35
00:01:56,490 --> 00:01:57,807
About twenty years later,

36
00:01:57,833 --> 00:02:02,450
Giuseppe Peano used series like it
to solve exactly these tangled systems,

37
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:06,400
while Sophus Lie built a theory
of continuous symmetry on the same idea.

38
00:02:06,450 --> 00:02:09,543
Today it runs quietly
inside flight software,

39
00:02:09,593 --> 00:02:14,350
robot controllers, circuit simulators,
drug-dosing models, and quantum computers.

40
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:16,400
So what does it mean?

41
00:02:16,450 --> 00:02:19,860
The obvious guess is to raise
e to each entry on its own.

42
00:02:19,910 --> 00:02:23,660
Try it on a small matrix,
with pi and minus pi off the diagonal.

43
00:02:23,710 --> 00:02:26,230
Exponentiate every entry, use the result

44
00:02:26,280 --> 00:02:29,910
to move the plane, and everything
gets flattened onto a single line.

45
00:02:29,960 --> 00:02:33,070
Whatever solves those equations,
it isn't this.

46
00:02:33,420 --> 00:02:35,770
The real answer is far nicer.

47
00:02:35,930 --> 00:02:40,970
e to this matrix is minus the identity:
every point swings around to the opposite

48
00:02:41,020 --> 00:02:43,130
side, a clean half turn.

49
00:02:43,180 --> 00:02:45,530
If you've met e to the i pi equals

50
00:02:45,580 --> 00:02:48,910
minus one, this is the same
fact in a different outfit.

51
00:02:49,700 --> 00:02:51,730
Here's the picture I'd
like you to leave with.

52
00:02:51,780 --> 00:02:56,470
A matrix can describe motion,
a velocity for every point in space.

53
00:02:56,690 --> 00:03:00,320
e to the matrix is where everything
ends up once that motion has run.

54
00:03:00,370 --> 00:03:02,830
And along the way,
one of the most familiar

55
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:05,150
rules of exponents is going to break.

56
00:03:05,540 --> 00:03:07,590
We'll start on a single number line,

57
00:03:07,640 --> 00:03:10,700
and finish with the whole
planet turning under your feet.

58
00:03:15,020 --> 00:03:18,270
Let's go back to the simplest
case and look at it sideways.

59
00:03:18,390 --> 00:03:22,140
Give every point on the number line
a velocity equal to where it sits.

60
00:03:22,190 --> 00:03:25,030
The point at two heads right at speed two.

61
00:03:25,080 --> 00:03:28,250
The point at minus one
drifts left at speed one.

62
00:03:28,300 --> 00:03:29,880
Zero stays put.

63
00:03:30,610 --> 00:03:32,380
Press play for one second.

64
00:03:32,630 --> 00:03:37,370
The point at one lands on e.
The point at two lands on two e.

65
00:03:37,420 --> 00:03:40,253
Everything got multiplied
by the same number.

66
00:03:40,253 --> 00:03:43,690
So e is the stretch you get
from one second of this flow.

67
00:03:44,370 --> 00:03:49,950
Speed the flow up by r, and t seconds
of it multiply everything by e to the r t.

68
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:54,760
Make r negative, and the line drains
toward zero, like the coffee's extra heat.

69
00:03:55,520 --> 00:03:59,390
You can also build that flow from small
steps, the way Bernoulli's banker did.

70
00:03:59,440 --> 00:04:01,950
Split the second into n pieces.

71
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:05,550
In each one, a point moves
by its velocity times the step,

72
00:04:05,600 --> 00:04:08,550
so it's multiplied by one plus one over n.

73
00:04:08,630 --> 00:04:12,890
Do that n times, and as n grows,
you close in on the true flow.

74
00:04:12,940 --> 00:04:16,290
Multiply it all out,
and the familiar series appears.

75
00:04:16,830 --> 00:04:23,250
So e to the x has three faces: a flow,
a pile of tiny steps, and an infinite sum.

76
00:04:23,350 --> 00:04:25,640
All three make the jump to matrices.

77
00:04:27,330 --> 00:04:28,820
Now add a dimension.

78
00:04:28,870 --> 00:04:30,990
A point in the plane is a vector,

79
00:04:31,040 --> 00:04:33,860
and a matrix turns vectors
into other vectors.

80
00:04:33,910 --> 00:04:39,460
So let it hand out velocities:
at the point v, the velocity is A times v.

81
00:04:40,130 --> 00:04:41,520
Here's a matrix to play with.

82
00:04:41,570 --> 00:04:44,850
It turns every vector a quarter
turn counterclockwise.

83
00:04:44,900 --> 00:04:46,690
As a velocity, that means

84
00:04:46,740 --> 00:04:50,470
every point moves at right angles
to the line joining it to the center.

85
00:04:51,090 --> 00:04:53,980
Now drop in a whole grid,
and watch the lines.

86
00:04:54,030 --> 00:04:57,100
They stay straight,
parallel, and evenly spaced.

87
00:04:57,150 --> 00:04:58,640
That's no coincidence.

88
00:04:58,680 --> 00:05:00,467
The velocity rule is linear,

89
00:05:00,493 --> 00:05:04,400
so if two points flow, their sum
flows to the sum of where they went.

90
00:05:04,460 --> 00:05:06,150
At every moment, the flow has

91
00:05:06,200 --> 00:05:11,080
done a linear transformation to the plane,
and a linear transformation is a matrix.

92
00:05:11,430 --> 00:05:14,330
That matrix is e to the t A.

93
00:05:14,440 --> 00:05:17,940
If you keep one definition
from this video, make it this one:

94
00:05:18,180 --> 00:05:23,430
e to the t A is where the flow of A has
carried every point after time t.

95
00:05:23,480 --> 00:05:27,670
A is the velocity.
e to the t A is the trip.

96
00:05:28,210 --> 00:05:29,780
Its numbers are easy to read.

97
00:05:29,830 --> 00:05:35,570
Wherever i-hat lands is the first column,
and wherever j-hat lands is the second.

98
00:05:35,570 --> 00:05:37,800
Here, both have turned through an angle t,

99
00:05:37,840 --> 00:05:40,750
so the columns fill up
with cosines and sines.

100
00:05:41,240 --> 00:05:44,850
Before I let it run any further:
where will the grid be when the flow

101
00:05:44,900 --> 00:05:46,900
has run for exactly pi?

102
00:05:50,100 --> 00:05:51,450
Halfway around.

103
00:05:51,500 --> 00:05:54,677
i-hat points left, and j-hat points down.

104
00:05:54,727 --> 00:05:57,950
That's minus the identity,
the answer from the start.

105
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:02,950
e to the i pi equals minus one is nothing
more mysterious than a half turn.

106
00:06:04,530 --> 00:06:07,920
Pictures are lovely,
but sooner or later you want numbers.

107
00:06:07,960 --> 00:06:09,950
So let's borrow Bernoulli's trick.

108
00:06:10,350 --> 00:06:11,540
A short time step,

109
00:06:11,580 --> 00:06:16,230
of length t over n, moves a point
v by its velocity times the step.

110
00:06:16,280 --> 00:06:21,120
So the new point is the identity
plus t A over n, applied to v.

111
00:06:21,170 --> 00:06:22,540
Do that n times,

112
00:06:22,580 --> 00:06:26,730
and you've applied the identity
plus t A over n, to the nth power.

113
00:06:27,340 --> 00:06:32,190
With four steps, the path is a lopsided
square that spirals away from the circle,

114
00:06:32,240 --> 00:06:35,560
because a straight step always
lands a little outside a curve.

115
00:06:35,830 --> 00:06:39,240
Eight steps do better.
Thirty-two hug the circle.

116
00:06:39,280 --> 00:06:41,660
In the limit, the error disappears.

117
00:06:42,080 --> 00:06:44,060
Now expand that power.

118
00:06:44,100 --> 00:06:45,490
Each term comes from choosing

119
00:06:45,540 --> 00:06:49,320
which of the n steps used A,
while the rest used the identity.

120
00:06:49,430 --> 00:06:51,340
One step: n choices,

121
00:06:51,380 --> 00:06:54,780
each weighing t over n,
so they add up to t A.

122
00:06:55,000 --> 00:07:00,540
Two steps: about n squared over two pairs,
giving t squared, A squared, over two.

123
00:07:00,590 --> 00:07:04,370
Three steps bring a three factorial,
because the same three nudges could

124
00:07:04,420 --> 00:07:06,380
arrive in six different orders.

125
00:07:06,710 --> 00:07:09,220
In the limit, that's the series.

126
00:07:09,270 --> 00:07:11,930
e to the A is the identity, plus A,

127
00:07:11,980 --> 00:07:17,500
plus A squared over two factorial, plus
A cubed over three factorial, on forever.

128
00:07:17,550 --> 00:07:19,507
It's the series for e to the x,

129
00:07:19,533 --> 00:07:23,370
with a matrix in place of the number,
and the identity in place of one.

130
00:07:23,710 --> 00:07:29,440
Those powers are matrix powers.
A squared means do A, then do A again.

131
00:07:29,490 --> 00:07:32,810
Watch them as arrows.
The first term is v.

132
00:07:32,860 --> 00:07:35,130
The next is A v, its velocity.

133
00:07:35,180 --> 00:07:37,460
Each one after that is the last,

134
00:07:37,500 --> 00:07:40,610
pushed through A again and divided
by the next whole number.

135
00:07:40,660 --> 00:07:44,500
Tip to tail, they swing out,
overshoot, and spiral back in,

136
00:07:44,540 --> 00:07:47,150
landing exactly where the flow carried v.

137
00:07:47,660 --> 00:07:52,267
For the half turn, the terms grow
at first, but factorials always win.

138
00:07:52,293 --> 00:07:55,370
The terms collapse,
and the total settles on minus one.

139
00:07:55,420 --> 00:07:59,830
That's true for any square matrix:
the series always converges.

140
00:08:00,180 --> 00:08:04,930
That's why the element-wise guess never
stood a chance: A squared mixes every

141
00:08:04,980 --> 00:08:06,870
entry with every other one.

142
00:08:08,650 --> 00:08:12,990
Some matrices are easy to exponentiate,
and they show how the rest work.

143
00:08:13,700 --> 00:08:15,980
A diagonal matrix is the easiest.

144
00:08:16,030 --> 00:08:19,410
Its flow is two number lines
minding their own business,

145
00:08:19,460 --> 00:08:22,103
growing sideways and shrinking vertically.

146
00:08:22,153 --> 00:08:26,490
So e to it is diagonal too:
just e to each diagonal entry.

147
00:08:26,540 --> 00:08:31,110
It's the one time you exponentiate
entries, and only on the diagonal.

148
00:08:31,160 --> 00:08:32,940
The zeros stay zero,

149
00:08:32,990 --> 00:08:35,600
where the element-wise guess
would have made them ones.

150
00:08:36,270 --> 00:08:38,320
Most matrices aren't diagonal,

151
00:08:38,360 --> 00:08:42,490
but many hide diagonal directions inside:
their eigenvectors.

152
00:08:42,540 --> 00:08:43,820
Here's a saddle.

153
00:08:43,870 --> 00:08:47,420
Along one diagonal,
points grow at rate three halves.

154
00:08:47,470 --> 00:08:50,620
Along the other,
they shrink at rate one half.

155
00:08:50,880 --> 00:08:53,030
Every other point is a blend of the two,

156
00:08:53,080 --> 00:08:56,320
carried both ways at once,
along these curves.

157
00:08:56,360 --> 00:08:58,950
Find the eigenvectors,
and the exponential is just

158
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:01,820
e to each eigenvalue, in each direction.

159
00:09:02,530 --> 00:09:04,780
What if the eigenvalues are complex?

160
00:09:04,830 --> 00:09:07,130
This matrix has eigenvalues minus

161
00:09:07,180 --> 00:09:11,380
a quarter, plus or minus i,
and its flow is a spiral.

162
00:09:11,420 --> 00:09:13,860
The real part sets how fast things shrink,

163
00:09:13,900 --> 00:09:17,020
and the imaginary part
sets how fast they turn.

164
00:09:17,020 --> 00:09:20,420
Complex eigenvalues are how
a real flow rotates.

165
00:09:21,030 --> 00:09:24,740
And some matrices don't have enough
eigenvectors, like this shear.

166
00:09:24,790 --> 00:09:28,693
Its velocity is horizontal,
and grows with height.

167
00:09:28,693 --> 00:09:32,610
Square it and you get zero,
so the series stops after two terms:

168
00:09:32,660 --> 00:09:36,260
e to the t N is just
the identity plus t N.

169
00:09:36,910 --> 00:09:39,140
One last pattern ties them together.

170
00:09:39,460 --> 00:09:43,040
Drop a small patch of points
into the flow and watch its area.

171
00:09:43,300 --> 00:09:47,510
In the spiral, it shrinks.
In the shear, what do you think happens?

172
00:09:50,150 --> 00:09:54,250
Nothing.
It leans over, but its area never changes.

173
00:09:54,350 --> 00:09:58,020
The determinant
of e to the A is e to the trace of A,

174
00:09:58,070 --> 00:09:59,660
the sum of its diagonal.

175
00:09:59,710 --> 00:10:04,680
The trace measures how much the field
spreads out at each point, its divergence.

176
00:10:04,730 --> 00:10:09,300
Rotations and shears have zero trace,
so they never change area.

177
00:10:10,900 --> 00:10:14,610
For ordinary numbers, there's
a rule you've used a thousand times:

178
00:10:14,660 --> 00:10:18,710
e to the a plus b equals e to the a,
times e to the b.

179
00:10:18,930 --> 00:10:20,800
Matrices refuse.

180
00:10:21,420 --> 00:10:22,840
Take two shears.

181
00:10:22,890 --> 00:10:26,280
A slides things sideways,
and B slides them up and down.

182
00:10:26,330 --> 00:10:30,310
Run A for one unit of time,
then B, and the unit square

183
00:10:30,360 --> 00:10:32,550
becomes this parallelogram.

184
00:10:32,600 --> 00:10:35,340
Run B first, then A,
and you get a different one.

185
00:10:35,390 --> 00:10:38,307
Run both at once, adding their velocities,

186
00:10:38,333 --> 00:10:42,880
and you get e to the A plus B:
a third shape, unlike either.

187
00:10:42,920 --> 00:10:46,540
All three keep the area at one,
but they're genuinely different.

188
00:10:46,860 --> 00:10:48,560
The series says why.

189
00:10:48,800 --> 00:10:54,780
Multiply e to the A by e to the B, and you
get A B among the second-order terms.

190
00:10:54,780 --> 00:10:59,270
Expand e to the A plus B instead,
and that term becomes half of A B,

191
00:10:59,320 --> 00:11:00,810
plus half of B A.

192
00:11:00,860 --> 00:11:04,280
They match only if A B equals B A.

193
00:11:04,330 --> 00:11:08,750
The difference, A B minus B A,
is called the commutator.

194
00:11:09,300 --> 00:11:10,460
And you can watch it.

195
00:11:10,650 --> 00:11:15,430
Run A forward for a moment, then B,
then A backward, then B backward.

196
00:11:15,480 --> 00:11:18,260
If they commuted,
you'd be back where you started.

197
00:11:18,400 --> 00:11:19,200
You aren't.

198
00:11:19,250 --> 00:11:21,470
The plane comes back squashed one way

199
00:11:21,520 --> 00:11:24,640
and stretched the other,
by exactly the commutator.

200
00:11:24,690 --> 00:11:28,460
Two shears in a loop make a stretch
that neither one contains.

201
00:11:28,510 --> 00:11:32,480
It's the same reason you can parallel
park: drive and steer in a loop,

202
00:11:32,530 --> 00:11:34,280
and the car moves sideways.

203
00:11:34,930 --> 00:11:38,310
So the old rule survives exactly
when the matrices commute.

204
00:11:38,360 --> 00:11:41,020
Shrinking toward the center
commutes with turning,

205
00:11:41,060 --> 00:11:45,720
which is why that spiral split so neatly
into a shrink, times a rotation.

206
00:11:47,400 --> 00:11:49,000
Now, back to that pill.

207
00:11:49,200 --> 00:11:51,290
One number for the medicine
in your stomach,

208
00:11:51,340 --> 00:11:53,290
another for the medicine in your blood.

209
00:11:53,340 --> 00:11:55,660
The stomach empties at a steady rate.

210
00:11:55,710 --> 00:11:59,120
The blood gains what the stomach loses,
and your body clears it

211
00:11:59,160 --> 00:12:01,050
in proportion to how much is there.

212
00:12:01,100 --> 00:12:04,160
As a matrix, the whole story is one line:

213
00:12:04,210 --> 00:12:09,160
the state after t hours is e to the t A,
times the starting state.

214
00:12:09,840 --> 00:12:14,380
Each molecule makes its own random hop,
but the crowd follows that exponential.

215
00:12:14,430 --> 00:12:17,010
The level in your blood climbs, peaks,

216
00:12:17,060 --> 00:12:21,050
and fades, and the two eigenvalues
are the two rates behind the curve:

217
00:12:21,100 --> 00:12:23,920
how fast you absorb,
and how fast you clear.

218
00:12:24,570 --> 00:12:28,977
Now the most beautiful case,
and it's happening to you right now.

219
00:12:29,027 --> 00:12:31,670
The Earth spins about
an axis through its poles,

220
00:12:31,720 --> 00:12:35,940
and each point moves at right angles
to both the axis and its own position.

221
00:12:35,990 --> 00:12:37,790
That's a cross product.

222
00:12:37,840 --> 00:12:39,040
Near the equator,

223
00:12:39,080 --> 00:12:42,600
you're being carried along at more
than sixteen hundred kilometers an hour.

224
00:12:42,650 --> 00:12:46,040
At a pole, you'd just turn on the spot.

225
00:12:46,220 --> 00:12:49,767
That cross product is a matrix:
zeros down the diagonal,

226
00:12:49,793 --> 00:12:53,530
and the axis coordinates around them,
each paired with its own negative.

227
00:12:53,600 --> 00:12:54,730
Call it K.

228
00:12:54,780 --> 00:12:59,570
Every point's velocity is the spin rate,
times K, times its position.

229
00:12:59,870 --> 00:13:01,160
Exponentiate it.

230
00:13:01,350 --> 00:13:05,430
K cubed is minus K,
so the infinite series folds up.

231
00:13:05,480 --> 00:13:10,000
The odd powers collapse into plus
or minus K, the even ones into plus

232
00:13:10,040 --> 00:13:14,490
or minus K squared, and the sine
and cosine series appear on their own.

233
00:13:14,540 --> 00:13:18,480
